1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,170
March 1974. Fort Worth, Texas.

2
00:00:05,150 --> 00:00:08,710
Detectives sit down across from a 27 -year -old man named Glenn McCurley.

3
00:00:10,010 --> 00:00:11,270
They ask him about a gun,

4
00:00:11,910 --> 00:00:16,990
a .322 Ruger pistol, the same model as a magazine dropped at a murder scene two weeks

5
00:00:16,990 --> 00:00:20,550
earlier. He says it was stolen from his truck.

6
00:00:21,230 --> 00:00:22,490
He agrees to a polygraph.

7
00:00:23,270 --> 00:00:27,270
He passes it. Detectives cross his name off the list.

8
00:00:27,630 --> 00:00:32,510
The file goes cold. It would stay cold for 46 years,

9
00:00:33,030 --> 00:00:37,350
seven months, and no one would look at his name again until a laboratory did it for them.

10
00:00:38,670 --> 00:00:39,670
You're watching Criminal Record.

11
00:00:41,310 --> 00:00:45,850
This is the murder of Carla Walker and the record of a man the police already had and

12
00:00:45,850 --> 00:00:49,370
let go. Chapter 1. The Parking Lot.

13
00:00:51,310 --> 00:00:55,410
February 17th, 1974. 17 -year -old Carla Walker,

14
00:00:55,970 --> 00:00:57,770
a cheerleader at Weston Hills High School,

15
00:00:58,050 --> 00:00:59,590
goes to a dance with her boyfriend,

16
00:01:00,050 --> 00:01:05,250
Rodney McCoy. Afterward, the two of them sit in his car in the parking lot of the Brunswick

17
00:01:05,250 --> 00:01:08,210
Ridgelea Bowl, a bowling alley on the west side of the city.

18
00:01:09,530 --> 00:01:11,570
A man forces the car door open.

19
00:01:12,190 --> 00:01:14,470
He beats Rodney unconscious with a pistol.

20
00:01:15,430 --> 00:01:20,010
Carla screams. He drags her out of the car and disappears into the dark with her.

21
00:01:21,290 --> 00:01:23,490
At the scene, police recover two things,

22
00:01:24,230 --> 00:01:27,630
Carla's purse, and a magazine dropped from the attacker's gun.

23
00:01:29,090 --> 00:01:31,230
Three days later, on February 20th,

24
00:01:31,490 --> 00:01:34,770
Carla Walker's body is found in a drainage culvert near Lake Benbrook,

25
00:01:35,190 --> 00:01:37,070
roughly 30 minutes south of the city.

26
00:01:37,530 --> 00:01:41,650
The record states she survived approximately two days after the abduction and that the

27
00:01:41,650 --> 00:01:43,330
cause of death was strangulation.

28
00:01:44,270 --> 00:01:46,250
Chapter 2. The Interview.

29
00:01:47,850 --> 00:01:50,650
The dropped magazine becomes the case's one real lead.

30
00:01:51,290 --> 00:01:52,490
It fits a specific model,

31
00:01:53,030 --> 00:01:54,930
a .22 caliber Ruger pistol.

32
00:01:56,390 --> 00:02:00,170
In early March, detectives find a man nearby who owns that exact gun.

33
00:02:00,750 --> 00:02:02,630
His name is Glen Samuel McCurley.

34
00:02:03,030 --> 00:02:06,530
He is 27 years old. They bring him in.

35
00:02:07,030 --> 00:02:11,170
McCurley tells them the gun was stolen out of his truck about six weeks earlier while

36
00:02:11,170 --> 00:02:16,430
he was out fishing. He says he never reported it because he was an ex -convict and didn't

37
00:02:16,430 --> 00:02:17,870
think police would believe him.

38
00:02:19,290 --> 00:02:20,330
Detectives note something else.

39
00:02:20,790 --> 00:02:24,670
McCurley was off work on the night of the 17th and his wife was out of town.

40
00:02:26,170 --> 00:02:29,570
Then they ask him to take a polygraph test downtown on the record.

41
00:02:30,090 --> 00:02:33,050
He agrees. He sits for it and he passes.

42
00:02:34,530 --> 00:02:36,410
On the strength of that single test,

43
00:02:36,810 --> 00:02:39,890
the task force removes Glen McCurley from its list of suspects.

44
00:02:39,890 --> 00:02:43,910
His name goes back into the file and the file goes cold.

45
00:02:45,350 --> 00:02:50,430
Chapter 3. 46 Years. No further investigative contact with Glen

46
00:02:50,430 --> 00:02:54,830
McCurley is documented anywhere in the public record between that polygraph in March

47
00:02:54,830 --> 00:03:00,210
1974 and the year 2020. Carla Walker's case sits,

48
00:03:00,470 --> 00:03:04,730
unsolved, in Fort Worth's cold case files for close to half a century.

49
00:03:04,730 --> 00:03:08,190
Her family ages. Investigators retire.

50
00:03:08,810 --> 00:03:12,090
The physical evidence, including that decades old gun magazine,

51
00:03:12,410 --> 00:03:15,530
waits in storage. Chapter 4.

52
00:03:15,950 --> 00:03:21,070
The Lab. In 2019, Fort Worth police send DNA

53
00:03:21,070 --> 00:03:25,790
recovered from Carla Walker's clothing decades earlier to a private forensic laboratory

54
00:03:25,790 --> 00:03:31,070
in Texas called Othram. Othram specializes in a method called forensic

55
00:03:31,070 --> 00:03:35,510
genetic genealogy. It rebuilds a usable genetic profile from degraded,

56
00:03:35,910 --> 00:03:40,350
decades old evidence, then searches public genealogy databases for distant relatives

57
00:03:40,350 --> 00:03:42,030
of whoever that profile belongs to.

58
00:03:43,370 --> 00:03:44,810
From that degraded sample,

59
00:03:45,230 --> 00:03:46,970
Othram's team builds a family tree.

60
00:03:47,530 --> 00:03:49,530
It leads them, branch by branch,

61
00:03:49,730 --> 00:03:51,930
toward one family in one city,

62
00:03:52,750 --> 00:03:55,970
Fort Worth. On September 4th,

63
00:03:55,990 --> 00:04:00,470
2020, investigators quietly collect a discarded item from outside a house.

64
00:04:01,030 --> 00:04:02,850
Its DNA is a familial match.

65
00:04:04,410 --> 00:04:07,110
Twelve days later, on September 16th,

66
00:04:07,150 --> 00:04:12,150
they obtain DNA directly connected to that same house and compare it to the profile recovered

67
00:04:12,150 --> 00:04:14,710
from Carla Walker's clothing in 1974.

68
00:04:15,710 --> 00:04:19,650
It is a match. The house belongs to Glenn Samuel McCurley,

69
00:04:20,050 --> 00:04:24,610
the same man detectives interviewed and cleared 46 years earlier.

70
00:04:26,110 --> 00:04:30,730
Chapter 5. The Arrest. On September 21st,

71
00:04:30,830 --> 00:04:35,030
2020, Fort Worth police arrest Glenn Samuel McCurley Jr.,

72
00:04:35,030 --> 00:04:38,290
now 77 years old, and charge him with capital murder.

73
00:04:39,510 --> 00:04:44,590
46 years, 7 months, and a handful of days have passed since a detective wrote

74
00:04:44,590 --> 00:04:46,290
his name in a file and closed it.

75
00:04:47,650 --> 00:04:51,010
By multiple accounts, when confronted with the DNA evidence,

76
00:04:51,590 --> 00:04:55,090
McCurley confesses. In May of 2021,

77
00:04:55,690 --> 00:04:57,870
prosecutors announced they will not seek the death penalty.

78
00:04:58,450 --> 00:05:01,350
McCurley is 78 years old by the time his case reaches trial.

79
00:05:02,770 --> 00:05:07,850
Chapter 6. The Plea. McCurley's capital murder trial opens August

80
00:05:07,850 --> 00:05:12,650
20th, 2021, at the Tim Curry Criminal Justice Center in Fort Worth,

81
00:05:12,850 --> 00:05:15,050
before State District Judge Elizabeth Beach.

82
00:05:16,430 --> 00:05:17,850
On the third day of trial,

83
00:05:18,190 --> 00:05:21,430
August 24th, McCurley withdraws his not guilty plea.

84
00:05:21,990 --> 00:05:25,590
In open court, he pleads guilty to the capital murder of Carla Walker.

85
00:05:26,670 --> 00:05:29,150
Because prosecutors were not seeking death,

86
00:05:29,530 --> 00:05:31,390
the plea triggers an automatic sentence.

87
00:05:32,030 --> 00:05:33,150
Judge Beach sentences him,

88
00:05:33,330 --> 00:05:35,810
on the spot, to life in prison without parole.

89
00:05:37,450 --> 00:05:41,570
McCurley later appealed, challenging the DNA evidence and his own statements to police.

90
00:05:42,310 --> 00:05:46,350
The second court of appeals reviewed every argument and affirmed his conviction in full.

91
00:05:47,950 --> 00:05:49,610
Chapter 7. The Record Afterward.

92
00:05:50,970 --> 00:05:54,470
At sentencing, Carla's sister addressed McCurley directly,

93
00:05:54,770 --> 00:05:58,430
telling him he had put the family through hell and that he should confess to any other

94
00:05:58,430 --> 00:06:00,410
crimes for the sake of other families.

95
00:06:02,050 --> 00:06:04,230
Her brother, Jim Walker, spoke too.

96
00:06:04,770 --> 00:06:07,930
We did our job to get Carla's perpetrator caught and brought to justice,

97
00:06:07,930 --> 00:06:11,230
he said, and it worked out the way it probably should have worked out.

98
00:06:12,190 --> 00:06:15,930
Glen Samuel McCurley died in prison on July 15th,

99
00:06:15,950 --> 00:06:18,930
2023, less than two years into his sentence.

100
00:06:20,370 --> 00:06:22,330
The case now carries a second legacy.

101
00:06:23,070 --> 00:06:25,290
Federal legislation named the Carla Walker Act,

102
00:06:25,570 --> 00:06:27,570
introduced by Senators Cornyn and Welch,

103
00:06:27,910 --> 00:06:32,990
proposes funding forensic genetic genealogy testing for other cold cases nationwide,

104
00:06:33,330 --> 00:06:37,070
using her name. Here is what the record actually shows,

105
00:06:37,290 --> 00:06:38,490
stripped of everything else.

106
00:06:38,970 --> 00:06:42,570
In 1974, a detective sat across from the man who did it,

107
00:06:42,770 --> 00:06:44,010
asked him the right question,

108
00:06:44,270 --> 00:06:46,010
and let a machine decide the answer.

109
00:06:47,310 --> 00:06:50,750
The gun magazine that named him sat in an evidence room for decades,

110
00:06:51,190 --> 00:06:55,030
waiting for a technology that didn't exist yet to catch up to a mistake that already

111
00:06:55,030 --> 00:06:58,150
had. His name was never lost.

112
00:06:58,710 --> 00:06:59,890
It was never even really hidden.

113
00:07:00,430 --> 00:07:02,770
It was simply written down in 1974,

114
00:07:03,330 --> 00:07:06,210
and nobody opened the file again for 46 years.

115
00:07:07,810 --> 00:07:10,830
If a name sitting quietly in a cold case file got to you,

116
00:07:11,050 --> 00:07:15,170
wait until you see the case where the record's own timeline breaks the official story

117
00:07:15,170 --> 00:07:18,970
apart, minute by minute. That full case file is on this channel.

118
